    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a per-city capacity rule to `POST /api/owners`: a create is rejected with **409** when the target city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (matching the existing `EnsureUniqueTelephone` / `EnsureUniqueHousehold` guards), I added:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** \u2014 a guard step that counts owners in the request's city and throws when the count is \u2265 50. Runs before `build`, so a full city is rejected before any owner is built or saved.\n2. **`CityCapacityExceededException`** (checked) + **`CityCapacityExceededExceptionHandler`** \u2014 the handler responds `409 CONFLICT` with `{\"error\": \"...\"}`, mirroring the existing duplicate handlers.\n3. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `...CityCapacityExceededException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n4. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted `ensureCityCapacity` into `owners.POST.yml` between the uniqueness checks and `build`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\nTo avoid duplicating logic, I extracted the case-insensitive city-counting that already lived privately in `AssignCustomerCode` into a shared **`Cities`** helper (analogous to the existing `Households` helper), and refactored `AssignCustomerCode` to reuse `Cities.countIn(...)` \u2014 so both the capacity check and the customer-code sequence count cities the same way, each class keeping a single responsibility.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409) passes, and the full suite is green.",
